>
  <div class="pagehead grain grain--dark">
    <p class="kicker">The Living Memory of Music</p>
    <h1>The Living Memory of Music</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">An oral history of American music, recorded in conversation with the people who made it.</p>
  </div>

  <article class="read grain lm-wide">
    <div class="col reveal">
      <p class="lede">The Living Memory of Music is an oral history of American music. We record the people who made it, in their own words, and keep what a record's credits leave out: who taught whom, what was passed on, and who was in the room.</p>

      <span class="kicker">Why it exists</span>
      <p>Most of the people who made this music have never been asked to tell their part of it in full. Many of them are still here. When they are gone, the stories go with them, unless someone asks. We are asking, and keeping what they say, credited to them, for good.</p>

      <span class="kicker">What it looks like</span>
      <p>Every person, song and moment in the Living Memory is a point of light. Type a name, and it takes you there.</p>
      <figure class="lm-clip">
        <video src="/assets/living-memory/lm-dolly.mp4" width="1120" height="525" poster="/assets/living-memory/lm-dolly.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="The Living Memory turns and flies in to Dolly Parton."></video>
        <figcaption>Finding Dolly Parton.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>The lines between the lights are the point. Each one is a connection someone told us about, with its source beside it: who taught whom, and who passed a song on.</p>
      <figure class="lm-clip">
        <video src="/assets/living-memory/lm-names.mp4" width="1120" height="525" poster="/assets/living-memory/lm-names.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="The whole Living Memory turning slowly, with names on."></video>
        <figcaption>The whole Living Memory with the names on: Mother Maybelle Carter, Woody Guthrie, Johnny Cash and hundreds more.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <span class="kicker">How it works</span>
      <p>We record people two at a time, talking to each other, because that's where the best stories come out. We call these conversations Pairs.</p>
      <ol class="lm-steps">
        <li><strong>You're invited.</strong> Someone who knows your work recommends you, and we write to you about one person in American music.</li>
        <li><strong>You choose who to talk with.</strong> Invite someone you'd like to talk with, or we'll pair you with someone who knew the same person or loved the same music.</li>
        <li><strong>We set the time.</strong> We find a time that works for you both and send one link. It works on a computer or a phone, and there's nothing to install or prepare.</li>
        <li><strong>You talk.</strong> About half an hour, longer if you like. Maybelle, our AI note-taker, is on the call. She shows the Living Memory on screen, and new lines appear as you talk.</li>
        <li><strong>You check your words.</strong> You see what was kept before anything is shared, and you fix anything that's wrong.</li>
        <li><strong>It's kept, with your name on it.</strong> Your story joins the Living Memory, and you get clips of your conversation to share.</li>
      </ol>
      <figure class="lm-clip">
        <img src="/assets/living-memory/pairs-call.jpg" width="1600" height="900" alt="An example Pairs call: two people on video on the right, Maybelle taking notes below them, the Living Memory shared on screen, and the words being said shown at the bottom." loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>What a Pairs call looks like. This is an illustration: the people and their words are examples.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <span class="kicker">Where your story goes</span>
      <p>What you say becomes part of the Living Memory: a line from you to the people you talked about, with your name on it. Up close, those lines read like a river, showing who passed the music on and to whom.</p>
      <figure class="lm-clip">
        <video src="/assets/living-memory/lm-river.mp4" width="1120" height="560" poster="/assets/living-memory/lm-river.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="Scrolling down one river of American song, from John A. Lomax onward."></video>
        <figcaption>One river, from John A. Lomax's field recordings down through the folk revival.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <span class="kicker">Our standards</span>
      <ul class="lm-standards">
        <li>Every story is credited to the person who told it.</li>
        <li>Nothing is shared until you have seen it.</li>
        <li>When you say "off the record," nothing you say is kept until you say "back on the record."</li>
        <li>When two people remember something differently, both accounts are kept, side by side.</li>
        <li>You can take back your part at any time, and it comes off the Living Memory.</li>
      </ul>

      <span class="kicker">Who's behind it</span>
      <p>The Living Memory of Music was founded by Jonathan Sexton, a writer and musician in Nashville at work on a book called The Sound of Something More. For questions, write to <a href="mailto:js@inthewld.com">js@inthewld.com</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
